        "topic": "Gas Laws and Kinetic Theory",
        "question_type": "Practical",
        "exam_year": "2001",
        "question_text": "<p>A gas occupies a volume of $500 cm^3$ at a pressure of $760 mmHg$. What will be its volume if the pressure is increased to $1520 mmHg$ at constant temperature?</p>",
        "question_image": null,
        "answer_image": null,
        "explanation": "<p>Using Boyle's Law, $P_1V_1 = P_2V_2$, where $P_1 = 760 mmHg$, $V_1 = 500 cm^3$, and $P_2 = 1520 mmHg$. Solving for $V_2$:<br>$V_2 = \\frac{P_1V_1}{P_2} = \\frac{760 \\times 500}{1520} = 250 cm^3$.</p>",
        "reference_passage": null,
        "novel": null,
        "comments_count": 0,
        "options": [],
        "_page_num": 1,
        "_page_idx": 4
    },
